        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most consumer electric pressure washers last roughly 3 to 7 years in typical home use, which works out to a few hundred hours of actual run time rather than thousands. Budget units with a universal motor and a sealed axial pump sit at the low end, while machines with an induction motor and good care can run well past that. The motor sets the ceiling, but the pump usually fails first, most often from freeze damage, running dry or long stretches idling with the trigger released. Those three causes are preventable, which is why two identical machines can reach very different ends.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Years Does an Electric Pressure Washer Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a homeowner who washes the car, the patio, the deck and the siding a handful of times a year, a typical electric pressure washer lasts somewhere around 3 to 7 seasons. A cheap unit used hard and stored badly can quit in one or two. A well-built machine with an induction motor, stored indoors and winterized properly, can keep going for a decade. The spread is wide because the machines vary a lot, and so does the way people treat them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Years are a misleading measure, though. A pressure washer only wears while it runs, and most home machines run for far fewer hours a year than people assume. Washing a car takes perhaps half an hour; a driveway or deck might take two or three. Add up a normal season and many homeowners log somewhere between 5 and 25 hours a year. A machine with a few hundred hours of pump life can therefore last a long time for one owner and a single summer for another.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Lifespan in Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3-7 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical home-use lifespan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100s</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Run hours, consumer pump</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1000s</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Run hours, quality triplex</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">#1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Killer: freeze damage</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The type of machine sets the ceiling. Most electric pressure washers under a few hundred dollars use a universal motor driving an axial pump, a combination designed to be light, compact and affordable rather than long-lived. Pricier electric units may use an induction motor, which runs cooler, quieter and longer. A very small number of heavy electric machines pair an induction motor with a triplex pump, and those are built for thousands of hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other big variable is avoidable damage. A surprising share of electric pressure washers die from something other than wear: a pump head cracked by ice over winter, seals destroyed by running without water, or overheating from being left running with the trigger released. If you avoid those, most machines reach the end of their designed life rather than an early one. If you do not, the motor and pump type barely matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Are Run Hours a Better Measure Than Years?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pressure washer pump wears with every stroke. The pistons slide in and out through seals, the check valves open and close, and the bearings or wobble plate carry the load. None of that happens while the machine sits in the garage, apart from slow aging of rubber parts. So the best predictor of how much life remains is how many hours it has actually run, and under what conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers of consumer machines rarely publish an hour rating, but the industry broadly treats a consumer axial pump as good for a few hundred hours, with the cheapest designs at the bottom of that range. Commercial triplex pumps are generally built for thousands of hours, and their seals and valves are serviced on a schedule much like oil changes on an engine. Those figures are rough, but the gap between them is real and is the main reason professional machines cost what they do.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate your own use, think about jobs rather than calendar time. A typical session for a car is under an hour, a small patio perhaps one or two, and a full house wash or a large deck several. If you cleaned the car monthly through summer and did a deck and a driveway once a year, you might log 15 hours. At that pace even a modest pump has years of life in it, provided nothing else damages it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conditions change the math. Hours spent at full pressure with a narrow nozzle, hours with a starved water supply that makes the pump cavitate, or hours in hot weather with the machine idling in bypass all wear the pump faster than easy hours with a wide fan tip and a strong supply. Heavy-use owners, such as people who clean for neighbors or run a small detailing business, often find a consumer unit lasts a season or two and should be buying a better class of machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Does a Universal or Induction Motor Last Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Induction motors last longer, often by a wide margin. A universal motor, the type found in most budget electric pressure washers, uses carbon brushes riding on a commutator to deliver power to the spinning armature. It spins very fast, runs hot and loud, and the brushes wear away with use. It is compact and cheap, which is why it dominates the low end of the market, and it is perfectly adequate for occasional use.
          </p>

          <p>
            An induction motor has no brushes. It is heavier and costs more to build, but it runs at a lower, steady speed, typically around 3,400 to 3,450 RPM on a standard two-pole motor in the US, and it runs much cooler and quieter. With no brushes to wear out, the motor itself can outlast the pump on most machines. Our explainer on <a href="/induction-vs-universal-motor-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">induction vs universal motor pressure washers</a> covers how to tell which one a listing is hiding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The motor is not usually the first thing to go, even on a universal unit. In many cases the pump's seals or valves wear out, or a freeze cracks the pump head, before the brushes reach the end. But a universal motor does put a softer ceiling on the machine's life, and on a busy machine the motor can be the part that ends it, typically with sparking, a burning smell, intermittent running or a loss of power under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical supply matters to motor life on both types. A long or thin extension cord drops voltage, which makes the motor draw more current and run hotter. Many manuals limit extension cords to a short length of a specified heavy gauge, and some recommend adding more garden hose rather than more cord. Running from a shared circuit that also powers a freezer or a space heater can trip breakers and stresses the motor too. Check your manual's cord guidance before you buy one.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
