            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Does the Pump Type Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            More than anything else on the machine. Most electric pressure washers use an axial pump, sometimes called a wobble-plate or swash-plate pump. The motor turns an angled plate that pushes a ring of small pistons in and out, and the pump is often a sealed unit with an aluminum or plastic head. It is compact, light and inexpensive, and it is usually treated as a replaceable part rather than something to rebuild.
          </p>

          <p>
            Triplex pumps work differently. A crankshaft drives three plungers through seals, much like a small engine, and the crankcase runs in an oil bath. The plungers are often ceramic, the head is often brass, and the seals and valves are designed to be replaced with kits. That serviceability is the real advantage: a triplex pump is a long-term asset you maintain, while an axial pump is a consumable you eventually replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Triplex pumps rarely appear on consumer electric machines, because they are heavy and expensive, and the electric motors that can drive them at useful flow rates often need more power than a standard 120-volt outlet provides. For a homeowner, the practical choice is between a cheap axial pump and a better axial pump. Look for a metal pump head rather than plastic, a brand that sells seal kits and parts, and a pump with its own warranty term.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pump life is also tied to how the pump is matched to the job. Running at the top of a cheap pump's rated pressure for hours at a time wears it faster than using a machine with a little headroom. If you are choosing a new machine, our pages on <a href="/pressure-washer-psi-vs-gpm/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">PSI vs GPM</a> and <a href="/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what PSI you need</a> help you avoid buying a small machine and working it at its limit every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Part Usually Fails First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump and its small parts fail far more often than the motor. Leading the list is freeze damage. Water left inside the pump expands by roughly 9 percent as it freezes, which is enough to crack an aluminum or plastic pump head, split a manifold or break the unloader. A machine that worked perfectly in October and leaks from the pump body in April has usually been frozen, and on many budget machines the only fix is a replacement pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next come seals and valves. Piston seals wear with use and fail faster with heat, grit, dry running and cavitation. The early signs are water dripping from under the pump, weak pressure, or pulsing that does not go away when the water supply is good. Check valves can stick open from debris or wear, and the unloader valve, which diverts water back through the pump when the trigger is released, can stick or leak and cause surging or pressure loss.
          </p>

          <p>
            Accessories wear out as well, but they are cheap to replace. Nozzle tips erode and lose pressure over time, quick-connect O-rings crack and leak, hoses kink and burst, and trigger guns develop leaks. Treat these as consumables. A machine that has lost pressure is often fine apart from a worn nozzle or a leaking connector, so rule those out before you assume the pump is finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical faults make up the rest. The GFCI built into the plug or cord of most electric pressure washers can trip repeatedly when moisture gets into a connection, and the cord or plug can be damaged by being dragged or run over. A tripping GFCI is a safety device doing its job, so treat it as a warning rather than an annoyance. On universal motors, worn brushes and switch failures account for many motor complaints.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
