                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Supply, air, unloader, valves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check supply first, then valves</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lost pressure, pump fine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worn nozzle or leaking fittings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New nozzle or O-rings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Motor sparking or cutting out</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushes, heat, voltage drop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shorter cord, service or replace</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Make an Electric Pressure Washer Last Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winterize it every year if you live anywhere that freezes. Disconnect the hose and gun, run the machine briefly to push out water, then draw a pump antifreeze or pump saver product through it as the manual describes, and store it somewhere above freezing. This one habit prevents the single most common way these machines die. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to winterize a pressure washer</a> covers the process for electric and gas units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never run it without water. Connect the hose, turn the faucet fully on, and squeeze the trigger until a steady stream of water comes out of the gun before you switch on the motor. That purges air and makes sure the pump is lubricated from the first stroke. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-can-a-pressure-washer-run-without-water/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long a pressure washer can run without water</a> explains why even a short dry start is costly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not leave it idling with the trigger released. On machines without an automatic total stop system, the motor keeps running and the water recirculates inside the pump, heating up quickly. Many manuals say not to leave it that way for more than a couple of minutes. If you are moving a ladder or repositioning, turn the machine off. Machines with total stop shut the motor down when you release the trigger, which helps, but they still benefit from being switched off during long breaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give it a good water supply. A garden hose that is too narrow or too long, a faucet only partly open, or a weak household supply makes the pump cavitate, which erodes valves and seals while you work. Clean the inlet screen regularly, flush the hose before connecting, and keep debris out of the inlet. Use detergents made for pressure washers, and rinse the detergent system with clean water afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store it properly between uses. Keep it out of direct sun and rain, coil the hose without tight kinks, cap the inlet and outlet to keep dirt and insects out, and lubricate the quick-connect O-rings occasionally with a silicone grease. None of this is time consuming, and together these habits can easily double the useful life of a budget machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Should You Repair It, and When Should You Replace It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the cheap parts. A lost-pressure complaint is often a worn nozzle, a blocked inlet screen or a leaking quick-connect, all of which cost very little to fix. Pulsing is usually a water supply problem first. Our guides on <a href="/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">a pressure washer not building pressure</a> and <a href="/why-is-my-pressure-washer-pulsing/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why a pressure washer pulses</a> walk through the cheap checks before anyone mentions a pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the warranty before you spend anything. Many consumer electric pressure washers carry a limited warranty of roughly one to three years, and some premium lines offer longer. Some warranties cover the pump for a shorter term than the rest of the machine, and most exclude freeze damage and running dry. Keep the receipt and register the product, because those details decide whether a claim is accepted.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a budget electric machine, a failed pump is usually where repair stops making sense. A replacement pump assembly, if you can find one, often costs a large fraction of a new machine, and the motor that drove it has also aged. If the machine cost under a couple of hundred dollars and the pump is cracked or worn out, replacement is usually the better use of money.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a mid-range or premium machine with an induction motor, a pump rebuild or replacement can be worth it, especially if the brand sells parts and the motor is in good shape. The same goes for any machine with a triplex pump, where seal and valve kits are routine maintenance. If you are replacing, our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washer guide</a> focuses on the motor, pump and build details that predict how long a machine will last.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fix the cheap parts; replace the cheap pump.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Nozzles, O-rings, screens and hoses are consumables. A cracked or worn-out pump on a budget machine is usually the end of the machine.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it wet over winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water trapped inside the pump expands as it freezes and can crack the pump head, manifold or unloader. It is the most common way a pressure washer dies in storage. Drain it, draw pump antifreeze through it after the last use, and keep it somewhere that stays above freezing until spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting the motor before water flows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Switching on before the pump is full of water runs the seals dry and fills the system with air. Even a brief dry start wears seals and can cause pulsing for the rest of the session. Connect the hose, open the faucet fully, squeeze the trigger until water flows, then start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving it idling between tasks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On machines without total stop, releasing the trigger sends water round and round inside the pump, where it heats quickly. Many manuals limit this to a couple of minutes. Turn the machine off whenever you move ladders, reposition the hose or take a break, and the pump will thank you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a long, thin extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Voltage drop from an undersized or overly long cord makes the motor draw more current and run hot, which shortens motor life and trips breakers. Follow the manual's cord gauge and length limits, plug directly into a suitable outlet, and add garden hose length instead of cord length when you need reach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a budget machine for heavy use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap universal-motor, axial-pump machine is designed for a few hours a month. Using it for hours every weekend, or for paid work, wears it out in a season or two. If you clean often, a machine with an induction motor and a serviceable pump costs more but works out cheaper per hour.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do electric pressure washers last longer than gas ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as a rule. Engines need more maintenance, but gas machines often come with sturdier pumps, which are what usually fail. A cheap electric unit and a cheap gas unit both tend to be limited by their axial pumps. The lifespan difference is driven more by pump quality, storage and care than by whether an engine or a motor turns the pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours will a cheap electric pressure washer run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers rarely publish a figure, but consumer axial pumps are broadly treated as good for a few hundred hours, and the cheapest designs fall at the lower end. For a homeowner that is still several years of normal use. Freeze damage, running dry and long idling in bypass can cut that dramatically regardless of the pump's design life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth replacing the pump on an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a budget machine, usually not. A replacement pump often costs a large share of a new unit, assuming parts are available at all. On a mid-range machine with an induction motor, or anything with a triplex pump, a pump repair or seal kit can be worthwhile. Check parts prices and warranty terms before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my pressure washer leak from the pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A leak from the pump body usually means worn or damaged seals, or a crack from freezing. Seals wear with age and fail faster after heat, dry running or grit. A crack after winter storage points to freeze damage. A drip from a small valve on the pump during idling may be the thermal relief valve releasing hot water, which is normal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does pump saver or antifreeze really make a difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. It displaces water that would otherwise freeze and expand inside the pump, and many products also lubricate seals and help keep them from drying out in storage. In freezing climates it prevents the most common cause of pump failure. Use a product intended for pressure washer pumps and follow the manual's instructions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I store my pressure washer in a cold garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the garage stays above freezing, or if the pump has been winterized with pump antifreeze. A garage that dips below freezing can still crack an undrained pump. Many owners bring the machine into a basement or utility room for winter, which also protects plastic parts and seals from temperature swings and extreme cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty should I look for on an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at the pump coverage specifically, not just the headline term. Many consumer machines offer around one to three years, and some premium lines offer longer. Check whether the pump is covered for the full period, whether freeze damage and misuse are excluded, and whether you must register the product to activate the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

