        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Expect roughly three to seven years from a typical electric pressure washer in home use, which means a few hundred hours of run time on most consumer pumps. Budget universal-motor machines sit at the low end, induction-motor machines last longer, and true triplex pumps are in a different league entirely. In almost every case, the pump decides when the machine is finished, not the motor.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The good news is that the causes of early failure are mostly in your hands. Winterize it, never start it dry, switch it off rather than leaving it idling, give it a full water supply and follow the extension cord guidance in the manual. Do that and your machine is far more likely to wear out from use than to die from a single bad afternoon or a cold night.
          </p>
        </div>

